From 3dfd8e8ccc24b2698f6e0bf19ade70577812aa50 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Florian Stosse <1315-Harvester57@users.noreply.git.frama-c.com>
Date: Tue, 17 Nov 2026 18:26:52 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [e-acsl] monitor the arguments of format functions

With -e-acsl-validate-format-strings and without -e-acsl-full-mtracking,
every call to a format function aborted, even with a literal format
string:

  printf("lit %d\n", 1)
  printf: invalid format string (unallocated or unterminated)

The RTL replacement checks that the format string, and the strings and
pointers passed for "%s", "%p" and "%n", are in monitored memory, but the
memory tracking analysis did not know about these checks: it only forced
the monitoring of string literals passed to the functions replaced with
-e-acsl-replace-libc-functions. The format string, a "%s" argument that
is a local array, and so on were not monitored, and the checks failed.
This includes the example of the user manual (section "Format String
Vulnerabilities"), which is run without -M.

When -e-acsl-validate-format-strings is set, the analysis now monitors
the blocks of every pointer argument of a format function, string
literals included, as it does for the arguments of a function whose
formals must be monitored.

The default configuration of tests/format does not use
-e-acsl-full-mtracking, so its oracles now monitor these blocks. The new
test tests/format/printf_mtracking.c is run without
-e-acsl-full-mtracking in the dev configuration too.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
